<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pierre Bataillon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1810–1877</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pierre Bataillon was a French Marist missionary who led the Catholic mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallis-island">Wallis Island</a> from 1837. Within a few years the island's whole population had converted, and he became vicar apostolic of Central Oceania, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, in 1843. He built churches and schools and kept great influence over the kings of Uvea, shaping the close tie between church and monarchy that still marks Wallis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-bataillon.jpg" alt="Pierre Bataillon" width="320" height="369" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Emile Deschamps · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mgr_Bataillon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
